# 1. MECHANISM OF ACTION AND NERVE PHYSIOLOGY

> **Syllabus**: [[mmed_anaes_part_a_syllabus#core-anaesthetic-drugs|Part A: Pharmacology]] | [[MMed_Anaesthesiology_Part_B_Syllabus#c2-sedation-and-general-anaesthesia|Part B: Sedation & General Anaesthesia]]

## 1.2. VOLTAGE-GATED SODIUM CHANNEL BLOCKADE

**Local anesthetics block voltage-gated sodium channels, preventing action potential generation and conduction in neuronal tissue.**

* LAs reversibly bind to the intracellular alpha subunit of voltage-gated sodium channels `[A:1]`
  * Binding site is located within the inner pore of the channel `[A:2]`
  * Hydrophobic aromatic residues (phenylalanine at position 1,764 and tyrosine at position 1,771) are essential for drug binding `[A:2]`
  * These residues line an inner cavity approximately 11 Å apart, roughly the size of a local anesthetic molecule `[A:2]`
* LA binding prevents the influx of sodium ions, thus preventing an action potential from being reached `[A:1]`
  * Resting membrane potential and threshold potentials are not affected `[A:1]`
  * With increasing LA concentration, more sodium channels bind LA molecules and cannot conduct sodium ions `[A:1]`
* LA has greater affinity for sodium channels in the open or inactivated state than in resting state `[A:1]`
  * Depolarization (open and inactivated states) favors LA binding `[A:1]`
  * LA binding is greater when nerve fibers are firing and depolarizing frequently than with infrequent depolarizations (use-dependent block) `[A:1]`
* Effects of local anesthetic block on nerve conduction:
  * Threshold for excitation and impulse conduction increases `[A:1]`
  * Rate of rise and magnitude of the action potential decreases `[A:1]`
  * Impulse conduction velocity slows `[A:1]`
  * At sufficient LA concentrations, action potentials can no longer be generated and impulse propagation is abolished `[A:1]`
* Alternative mechanism: 'membrane expansion' may also play a role `[A:157]`
  * Unionized drug dissolves into the phospholipid membrane
  * May cause swelling of the Na+ channel/lipoprotein matrix
  * Results in channel inactivation `[A:157]`

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** Use-dependent block explains why active, rapidly firing pain neurons are often blocked before resting neurons, providing analgesia before complete anesthesia `[A:1]`.

**Bottom Line:** Local anesthetics block voltage-gated sodium channels by binding to a specific site within the inner pore, preventing sodium influx and action potential generation. This blockade is enhanced during high-frequency nerve firing (use-dependent block). Alternative mechanisms like membrane expansion may also contribute to the blocking effect.

## 1.3. NERVE ANATOMY AND PHYSIOLOGY [PART A]

Tags: #pA_physio #pB_C2

**Nerve anatomy and physiology determine local anesthetic spread, uptake, and effect.**

### 1.3.1. NERVE STRUCTURE

* Peripheral nerves contain both afferent and efferent fibers, bundled into fascicles within three tissue layers `[A:3]`:
  * **Endoneurium**: Loose connective tissue surrounding individual nerve fibers `[A:3]`
  * **Perineurium**: Dense layer of collagenous connective tissue surrounding each fascicle `[A:3]`
  * **Epineurium**: Final layer of dense connective tissue encasing groups of fascicles `[A:3]`
* These tissue layers protect nerve fibers and act as barriers to passive diffusion of local anesthetics `[A:3]`

### 1.3.2. MYELINATION

* Myelinated nerves are surrounded by:
  * Schwann cells in the PNS `[A:3]`
  * Oligodendrocytes in the CNS `[A:3]`
* Myelin forms a concentrically wrapped lipid bilayer sheath interrupted at nodes of Ranvier `[A:3]`
  * Nodes of Ranvier contain densely clustered protein elements essential for transmission of neuronal signals `[A:3]`
  * Electrical signals are renewed at each node (saltatory conduction) `[A:4]`
* Unmyelinated nerves lack nodes of Ranvier `[A:3]`
  * Several nerve fibers may be simultaneously embedded within a single Schwann cell `[A:4]`
  * Conduction along unmyelinated fibers is relatively slow compared to myelinated fibers `[A:156]`
  * Maximum conduction velocity for myelinated fibers reaches 120 m/sec `[A:156]`

### 1.3.3. ACTION POTENTIAL GENERATION

* Neurons maintain a resting membrane potential of −60 to −70 mV `[A:5]` `[A:156]`
  * Maintained by Na⁺-K⁺-ATPase (electrogenic sodium-potassium pump) `[A:5]` `[A:156]`
  * Cell membrane is more permeable to K⁺ than Na⁺, resulting in negative intracellular potential `[A:5]` `[A:156]`
  * Ratio of intracellular to extracellular K+ affects the resting membrane potential `[A:156]`
  * Hypokalaemia increases (makes more negative) the resting membrane potential `[A:156]`
  * Na+ concentration has little effect when in the resting state due to impermeability `[A:156]`
* Action potentials involve:
  * Initial electrical or chemical triggers cause slow rise in membrane potential `[A:156]`
  * When threshold potential (about -50mV) is reached, voltage-sensitive Na+ channels open `[A:156]`
  * Rapid membrane depolarization from sodium influx `[A:7]` `[A:156]`
  * Na+ permeability increases dramatically and membrane potential reaches +30 mV `[A:156]`
  * Na+ channels then close and increased K+ efflux returns membrane potential to resting value `[A:156]`
  * Action potentials propagate as a wave along the nerve `[A:7]` `[A:157]`
  * A refractory period prevents retrograde spread `[A:7]` `[A:156]`
  * Na+/K+ ATPase restores concentration gradients, though total ions moved is small `[A:156]`

![Changes in Na+ and K+ conductance during the action potential](https://747b-138-75-252-16.ngrok-free.app/_served_images/LA/LA_LA_Untitled_9.png_7802d0.png_bf1acf.png)

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** Local anesthetics only penetrate about 1-2% of the injected dose into nerves due to the perineural barrier, explaining why much higher concentrations are needed clinically than in desheathed nerve experiments `[A:15]`.

**Bottom Line:** Understanding nerve anatomy is crucial for effective local anesthetic practice. Perineural barriers limit drug uptake while myelination and nerve size affect sensitivity to blockade. Action potentials result from precisely regulated ion fluxes across neuron membranes, and local anesthetics prevent action potential generation by blocking voltage-gated sodium channels.

## 1.4. NERVE FIBER CLASSIFICATION AND DIFFERENTIAL BLOCKADE [PART A]

Tags: #pA_physio #pB_C2 #pC_PC

**Nerve fibers of different types and sizes have varying sensitivity to local anesthetics.**

### 1.4.1. NERVE FIBER CLASSIFICATION

* Nerve fibers are classified by size, conduction velocity, and function `[A:4]`:

| Type | Diameter | Myelination | Conduction Velocity | Function |
|------|----------|-------------|---------------------|----------|
| A-alpha | 12-20 μm | Yes | 70-120 m/s | Motor, proprioception |
| A-beta | 5-12 μm | Yes | 30-70 m/s | Touch, pressure |
| A-gamma | 3-6 μm | Yes | 15-30 m/s | Muscle spindle |
| A-delta | 2-5 μm | Yes | 12-30 m/s | Pain, temperature, touch |
| B | <3 μm | Yes | 3-15 m/s | Pre-ganglionic autonomic |
| C | 0.4-1.2 μm | No | 0.5-2.3 m/s | Pain, temperature, post-ganglionic autonomic |

* General principles `[A:4]`:
  * Fibers with diameter >1 mm are typically myelinated
  * Larger diameter and presence of myelin = faster conduction velocity
  * Large myelinated A fibers are typically involved in motor and sensory functions requiring speed
  * Small unmyelinated C fibers have slower conduction velocity and relay pain, temperature, and autonomic functions

### 1.4.2. DIFFERENTIAL BLOCK

* Sensitivity of nerve fibers to LA inhibition is influenced by:
  * Axonal diameter: Smaller fibers are more sensitive than larger fibers of the same type `[A:21]`
  * Myelination: Myelinated fibers are more sensitive than unmyelinated fibers `[A:21]`
* Order of sensitivity to local anesthetics (most to least): B fibers > A fibers > C fibers `[A:21]`
  * Small myelinated fibers (B) are easiest to block with least surface area
  * Unmyelinated nerves (C) are most resistant because surface area of available channels to block is largest
* Clinical progression of block in human peripheral nerves: autonomic → sensory → motor `[A:21]`
* "Differential blockade" explains the ordered progression of sensory and motor deficits `[A:17]`:
  * Temperature sensation first (smallest fibers)
  * Followed by proprioception
  * Then motor function
  * Sharp pain
  * Light touch last (largest fibers)

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** Understanding differential block helps assess block adequacy - loss of cold sensation typically precedes surgical anesthesia, while preserved motor function doesn't necessarily indicate block failure `[B:4]`.

⚠️ **Safety Note:** The classic theory that smaller fibers are blocked first is not universally true - larger myelinated Aδ fibers (sharp pain) are preferentially blocked over small unmyelinated C fibers (dull pain), which has important implications for assessing block adequacy `[A:17]`.

**Bottom Line:** Different nerve fibers have varying sensitivity to local anesthetics, generally following the pattern: small myelinated (B) > large myelinated (A) > small unmyelinated (C) fibers. This explains the clinical progression of autonomic block before sensory block before motor block.

## 1.5. MOLECULAR MECHANISMS OF LOCAL ANESTHETICS [PART A]

Tags: #pA_pharm #pA_physio #pC_MK

**Local anesthetics block transmission of nerve impulses through direct interaction with voltage-gated sodium channels.**

### 1.5.1. VOLTAGE-GATED SODIUM CHANNELS

* Voltage-gated sodium channels are essential for action potential generation `[A:7]` `[A:156]`
* Structure `[A:8]`:
  * Principal α-subunit: Single-polypeptide transmembrane protein
  * Contains four homologous α-helical domains (D1 to D4) forming the channel pore
  * Controls ion selectivity, voltage-sensing, and inactivation
  * Contains phosphorylation sites for modulation by protein kinases
  * Auxiliary β-subunits: Short polypeptide proteins with a single transmembrane domain
  * Modulate channel expression, localization, and function
* Channel states `[A:8]`:
  * Resting/closed state: Predominant in absence of stimulus
  * Open state: Conformational change upon membrane depolarization allows sodium influx
  * Inactivated state: Channel becomes refractory to activation
	* Fast inactivation: Completes within milliseconds; sensitive to local anesthetics
	* Slow inactivation: Lasts seconds to minutes; resistant to local anesthetics
* Sodium channel isoforms `[A:11]`:
  * Nine isoforms (NaV 1.1 to NaV 1.9) have been identified
  * Tissue-specific expression (e.g., NaV 1.2 in CNS, NaV 1.6 at nodes of Ranvier)
  * NaV 1.7, 1.8, and 1.9 are found in nociceptors
  * NaV 1.5 is the primary cardiac sodium channel

### 1.5.2. MECHANISM OF LA-CHANNEL INTERACTION

* LAs reversibly bind the intracellular portion of voltage-gated sodium channels `[A:12]` `[A:157]`
  * QX-314 studies showed that permanently charged derivatives only block when applied intracellularly `[A:12]`
  * Hydrophobic interactions are primary binding forces `[A:19]`
  * Unionized lipid-soluble drug passes through phospholipid membrane `[A:157]`
  * In axoplasm, it is protonated and in this ionized form binds to Na+ channel `[A:157]`
  * Prevents channel from leaving inactive state `[A:157]`
* LA binding effects `[A:14]`:
  * Tonic blockade: Concentration-dependent decrease in peak sodium current
  * Use-dependent blockade: Repetitive stimulation leads to greater block at same concentration `[A:157]`
  * Degree of blockade in vitro is proportional to rate of stimulation due to attraction of local anesthetic to 'open' Na+ channels `[A:157]`
  * Competing theories for use-dependent block:
	* Modulated-receptor theory: LAs bind more avidly to open/inactivated channels
	* Guarded-receptor theory: Access to binding site increases with channel gating

![Mechanism of action of local anesthetics](https://747b-138-75-252-16.ngrok-free.app/_served_images/LA/LA_LA_Untitled_2.png_7156a3.png_1cee9a.png)

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** Variability in sodium channel isoforms explains why some patients experience different sensitivities to local anesthetics and why certain pain conditions (like erythromelalgia) involve sodium channel mutations `[A:11]`.

**Bottom Line:** Local anesthetics block nerve impulses by binding to the intracellular portion of voltage-gated sodium channels through hydrophobic interactions. This binding is enhanced by frequent channel activation (use-dependent block), making active pain fibers particularly susceptible. Membrane expansion theory may provide an additional mechanism of action.

