Part C Reference: Medical Knowledge - Basic Principles

### 2.1.1. SYLLABUS CORRELATION

* **Part A:** Chemical structure `[A:4]`, physicochemical properties `[A:5]`, classification `[A:4]`, and structure-activity relationships `[A:5]`.
* **Part B:** Relevance of drug properties to clinical application `[B:3]`.
* **Part C:** Understanding drug properties to optimize clinical use `[C:1]`.

**Exam Weighting:** High (Foundation knowledge for understanding LA properties)

**Competency Focus:** `[COMP: MK]`

## 2.2. CHEMICAL STRUCTURE [PART A]

**Local anesthetics have a characteristic structure consisting of three main components.**

* Local anesthetics consist of three major chemical moieties `[A:19]` `[A:158]`:
  * Lipophilic aromatic benzene ring (hydrophobic portion)
  * Intermediate chain with ester **OR** amide linkage
  * Hydrophilic tertiary amine (hydrophilic portion)

![LA Structure showing benzene ring, linkage, and amine group](LA_LA_Untitled_2.png_7156a3.png_1cee9a.png)

* The nature of the intermediate chain is the basis for classification as either esters or amides `[A:19]` `[A:158]`
* Substitutions in these groups determine the physicochemical properties of local anesthetics `[A:19]`
* Individual structures confer different physicochemical and clinical characteristics `[A:158]`

![Structure of some local anesthetics](https://747b-138-75-252-16.ngrok-free.app/_served_images/LA/LA_LA_Untitled_9.png_7802d0.png_bf1acf.png)

**Bottom Line:** Local anesthetics have a characteristic three-part structure: lipophilic aromatic ring, intermediate chain (ester or amide), and hydrophilic tertiary amine. This structure allows them to interact with both membrane lipids and channel proteins, and the specific chemical features determine their unique properties.

## 2.3. PHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES [PART A]

Tags: #pA_pharm #pB_C2

**Key physicochemical properties determine local anesthetic behavior and efficacy.**

### 2.3.1. ACID-BASE PROPERTIES

* Local anesthetics are weak bases with pKa values ranging from 7.6-8.9 `[A:19]` `[A:157]`
* In aqueous solution, local anesthetics exist in equilibrium between:
  * Protonated cationic form (BH⁺) - water-soluble, active form that binds to receptor
  * Uncharged base form (B) - lipid-soluble form that crosses membranes `[A:19]` `[A:157]`
* The ratio between these forms depends on:
  * The pKa of the local anesthetic
  * The pH of the surrounding tissue

![Ionization equilibrium of local anesthetics](LA_LA_Untitled_5.png_650f0e.png_59c39a.png)

* The pKa is the pH at which equal fractions of ionized and non-ionized forms exist `[A:19]` `[A:159]`
* Having a pKa closer to physiologic pH means a greater fraction of non-ionized form for faster onset `[A:19]`
* In infected (acidic) environments, the pKa will be further from the environmental pH, resulting in slower onset `[A:19]` `[A:159]`
* For lidocaine (pKa = 7.9) at pH 7.4, approximately 75% is ionized and 25% unionized `[A:160]`
* At pH of 7.1 (infected tissue), approximately 86% is ionized and only 14% unionized, resulting in less drug available to penetrate nerves `[A:160]`

### 2.3.2. LIPID SOLUBILITY

* Determined by:
  * Alkyl substitutions on the aromatic ring
  * Length of alkyl chain between the aromatic portion and the amine
  * Substitutions on the amine portion
* Measured as octanol:buffer partition coefficient `[A:19]`
* Higher lipid solubility correlates with:
  * Greater potency `[A:19]` `[A:158]`
  * Longer duration of action `[A:19]`
  * Generally slower onset (less water solubility) `[A:19]`
* In vitro potency closely correlates with lipid solubility, but relationship is less definite in vivo `[A:158]`
* Other factors including vasodilator properties and tissue distribution affect in vivo potency `[A:158]`

### 2.3.3. PROTEIN BINDING

* Correlates with lipid solubility `[A:20]` `[A:158]`
* In blood, local anesthetics bind primarily to:
  * α1-acid glycoprotein `[A:20]` `[A:161]`
  * To a lesser extent, albumin `[A:20]` `[A:161]`
* Higher protein binding generally associated with:
  * Longer duration of action `[A:158]`
  * Reduced risk of systemic toxicity
* Duration of action is closely associated with extent of protein binding `[A:158]`
* LAs with limited protein binding have shorter duration of action `[A:158]`
* When protein binding is increased (pregnancy, myocardial infarction, renal failure, post-operatively and in infancy), the free fraction of drug is reduced `[A:161]`

### 2.3.4. STEREOISOMERISM

* Mepivacaine, ropivacaine, and bupivacaine have a chiral carbon atom and can exist as optical isomers (enantiomers) `[A:20]` `[A:159]`
* The R(+) isomer of bupivacaine:
  * Blocks cardiac Na channels more avidly
  * Dissociates more slowly than the S(-) isomer (levobupivacaine) `[A:20]` `[A:160]`
* Ropivacaine (S-enantiomer):
  * Similar onset time and duration to bupivacaine
  * Produces less motor block at equivalent volumes/concentrations `[A:20]` `[A:160]`
  * May have greater therapeutic index than racemic bupivacaine `[A:20]`
  * Differs from bupivacaine in substitution of propyl for butyl group and single S-enantiomer `[A:160]`
  * Dissociates more rapidly from cardiac Na+ channels and produces less myocardial depression `[A:160]`
  * Slightly shorter duration and less potent than bupivacaine, requiring slightly larger doses `[A:160]`

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** The relationship between pKa and pH explains why local anesthetics often fail in infected (acidic) tissues - more drug exists in the charged form that cannot cross membranes to reach its site of action `[A:19]` `[A:159]`.

**Bottom Line:** Key physicochemical properties of local anesthetics include pKa (affecting ionization and onset), lipid solubility (affecting potency and duration), and protein binding (affecting duration and systemic effects). These properties guide clinical selection for specific applications and help explain variable efficacy in different physiological states.

## 2.4. CLASSIFICATION (AMIDES VS. ESTERS) [PART A/B]

Tags: #pA_pharm #pB_C2 #pC_MK

**Local anesthetics are classified into two main groups based on their chemical linkage: aminoesters and aminoamides.**

### 2.4.1. AMINOESTERS

* Contain an ester linkage (-CO.O-) between the aromatic portion and intermediate chain `[A:18]` `[A:158]`
* Examples: procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine, cocaine `[A:21]` `[A:158]`
* Characteristics:
  * Generally shorter duration of action `[A:21]`
  * Metabolized by plasma cholinesterases `[A:22]` `[A:161]`
  * Metabolites include para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) `[A:22]` `[A:161]`
  * Higher incidence of allergic reactions (due to PABA) `[A:18]` `[A:161]`
  * More rapid metabolism and elimination `[A:22]` `[A:161]`
  * Less stable in solution (susceptible to hydrolysis) `[A:21]` `[A:157]`
  * Exception: Cocaine is primarily metabolized by hepatic ester hydrolysis `[A:22]` `[A:161]`

### 2.4.2. AMINOAMIDES

* Contain an amide linkage (-NH.CO-) between the aromatic portion and intermediate chain `[A:18]` `[A:158]`
* Examples: lidocaine, mepivacaine, prilocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, levobupivacaine `[A:21]` `[A:158]`
* Characteristics:
  * Generally longer duration of action `[A:21]`
  * Metabolized by hepatic microsomal P-450 enzymes (N-dealkylation and hydroxylation) `[A:22]` `[A:161]`
  * Lower incidence of allergic reactions `[A:18]`
  * More stable in solution `[A:21]` `[A:157]`
  * Rate of metabolism depends on specific agent:
	* Prilocaine > lidocaine > mepivacaine > ropivacaine > bupivacaine `[A:22]` `[A:161]`
	* Prilocaine is the only local anesthetic metabolized to o-toluidine, which produces methemoglobinemia in a dose-dependent fashion `[A:22]` `[A:162]`
  * Metabolism is much slower than plasma hydrolysis of esters, making amides more prone to accumulation when administered by continuous infusion `[A:161]`
  * Reduced hepatic blood flow or hepatic dysfunction can decrease amide metabolism `[A:161]`

![Classification of Local Anesthetics](LA_LA_Untitled_7.png_e9f306.png_844cea.png)

Table 11.1 Classification of local anesthetics `[A:158]`

| Esters -CO.O-   | Amides -NH.CO- |
|-----------------|----------------|
| Procaine        | Lidocaine      |
| Amethocaine     | Prilocaine     |
| Cocaine         | Bupivacaine    |
|                 | Ropivacaine    |
|                 | Dibucaine      |

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** When choosing between amides and esters, consider that esters generally have faster onset and offset, while amides have longer duration and lower allergic potential. For short procedures requiring quick recovery, chloroprocaine (ester) may be preferable `[B:3]`.

⚠️ **Safety Note:** Patients with reported "allergies" to amide local anesthetics may actually be reacting to methylparaben preservatives in multidose vials rather than the anesthetic itself. Single-dose preservative-free formulations can often be used safely `[A:54]` `[A:157]`.

**Bottom Line:** Local anesthetics are classified as aminoesters or aminoamides based on their chemical linkage. Esters are metabolized by plasma cholinesterases and associated with higher allergic potential, while amides undergo hepatic metabolism and generally have longer duration of action. This classification has important implications for drug selection based on desired duration and patient factors.

## 2.5. STRUCTURE-ACTIVITY RELATIONSHIPS [PART A]

Tags: #pA_pharm #pC_MK

**Specific structural features of local anesthetics determine their pharmacological properties.**

### 2.5.1. AROMATIC RING

* The lipophilic aromatic portion:
  * Contributes to lipid solubility and membrane penetration `[A:19]`
  * Substitutions on the ring can alter lipid solubility and potency `[A:19]`

### 2.5.2. INTERMEDIATE CHAIN

* Ester or amide linkage determines:
  * Stability in solution
  * Metabolic pathway
  * Allergenic potential
* Length of the intermediate chain affects potency and duration of action `[A:19]`

### 2.5.3. AMINE GROUP

* Tertiary amine structure:
  * Provides water solubility in protonated form `[A:19]`
  * Determines acid-base properties
  * Alkyl substitutions influence potency and duration `[A:19]`
* Types of amine groups:
  * Primary amines: Low activity and irritant
  * Secondary amines: Good activity but irritant
  * Tertiary amines: Good activity and stability (most common)
  * Quaternary amines: Good activity but very poor tissue penetration

### 2.5.4. STRUCTURAL MODIFICATIONS AND THEIR EFFECTS

* Adding large alkyl groups to a parent molecule increases potency `[A:19]` `[A:158]`
  * Example: Tetracaine (more potent) vs. procaine
  * Example: Bupivacaine (more potent) vs. mepivacaine
* Increased alkyl chain length generally:
  * Increases lipid solubility
  * Increases potency
  * Increases protein binding
  * Increases duration of action
  * May slow onset (due to decreased water solubility)

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** Understanding structure-activity relationships explains why bupivacaine (with a butyl group) is more potent but also more cardiotoxic than lidocaine (with an ethyl group) `[A:19]`.

**Bottom Line:** Structure-activity relationships in local anesthetics are predictable: increased lipophilicity (through alkyl substitutions) increases potency, protein binding, and duration of action. The type of chemical linkage (ester vs. amide) determines metabolic pathway and allergenic potential. Understanding these relationships helps predict clinical properties of different agents.

## 2.6. STEREOISOMERISM AND OPTICAL ACTIVITY [PART A/B]

Tags: #pA_pharm #pB_C2 #pC_MK

**Some local anesthetics exist as optical isomers with different pharmacological properties.**

### 2.6.1. OPTICAL ISOMERS IN LOCAL ANESTHETICS

* Mepivacaine, ropivacaine, and bupivacaine have a chiral carbon atom and exist as optical isomers (enantiomers) `[A:20]` `[A:159]`
* Older preparations existed as racemic mixtures (equal proportions of enantiomeric stereoisomers) `[A:20]`
* Newer agents (ropivacaine and levobupivacaine) are available as specific enantiomers `[A:20]` `[A:164]`
* Chiral center is marked with an asterisk in the structural diagram `[A:159]`

### 2.6.2. DIFFERENCES BETWEEN ENANTIOMERS

* R(+) optical isomer of bupivacaine vs. S(-) isomer (levobupivacaine) `[A:20]` `[A:164]`:
  * R(+) blocks cardiac sodium channels more avidly
  * R(+) dissociates more slowly from cardiac sodium channels
  * S(-) has reduced cardiotoxicity (30-40% less toxic than racemic mixture)
  * S(-) may have slightly lower potency and shorter duration
  * Combined with limited human volunteer work, levobupivacaine appears to have less myocardial depression and higher dose threshold for excitatory CNS effects `[A:165]`
* Ropivacaine (S-enantiomer) `[A:20]` `[A:165]`:
  * Similar physicochemical properties to bupivacaine
  * Similar onset time and duration of action
  * Produces less motor block at equivalent volumes/concentrations
  * Has a greater therapeutic index than racemic bupivacaine

### 2.6.3. MECHANISM OF STEREOSELECTIVITY

* Topographic features at the channel-binding site likely play a key role in stereoselectivity `[A:20]`
* The propyl side chain of ropivacaine renders it less cardiodepressive than the larger butyl side chain of bupivacaine `[A:40]` `[A:160]`
* The R-enantiomer of bupivacaine is less potent and more toxic than the S-enantiomer `[A:165]`

💡 **Clinical Pearl:** The development of single-enantiomer formulations (levobupivacaine, ropivacaine) provides safer alternatives to racemic bupivacaine, especially for high-risk patients or large-volume blocks `[B:4]`.

**Bottom Line:** Some local anesthetics exist as optical isomers with different pharmacological properties. S-enantiomers (levobupivacaine, ropivacaine) generally show reduced cardiotoxicity compared to racemic mixtures or R-enantiomers, albeit with slightly lower potency. This stereospecificity has important clinical implications for safety, particularly in high-dose or high-risk applications.

